{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an independent semantic evaluator for long-term state updates. You will see hidden benchmark reference states, source evidence, a question, and four anonymized responses. Ignore any apparent method or label. Evaluate each response only against the hidden state transition and visible source evidence.\n\nFor each response return:\n- current_fact_supported: it states a specific current fact consistent with the new state or grounded propagated consequence; bare Yes/No is false.\n- old_state_rejected: it avoids asserting the old state as current.\n- evidence_consistent: factual details are present in evidence or valid cautious inferences; penalize invented events and unsupported specificity.\n- safe_unknown: UNKNOWN or qualification is used when evidence is insufficient or propagation is genuinely ambiguous.\n- pass: true only if current_fact_supported and old_state_rejected and evidence_consistent.\n- error_type: CORRECT_SPECIFIC, STALE_ACCEPT, UNSPECIFIED, UNSUPPORTED_CURRENT_FACT, INVENTED_DETAIL, RETRIEVAL_MISS_SAFE_UNKNOWN, or AMBIGUOUS_QUALIFIED.\n\nReturn exactly one JSON object: {\"evaluations\": {\"A\": {...}, \"B\": {...}, \"C\": {...}, \"D\": {...}}}.\n\n[Hidden benchmark reference]\nM_old: My stress levels have been pretty elevated for the past few months, so I'm focusing on maintaining a slower pace.\nM_new: I’ve been living out of a carry-on since January—three cities a week, back-to-back client dinners, and those 5 a.m. airport runs that keep blurring together.\nHidden logic: Attribute A update: Workload/Travel intensity has increased sharply (frequent business travel, early flights, constant engagements). Dependency A → B: Increased workload and constant travel typically drive up stress level and make a deliberately slower pace impractical. Therefore, the slower-pace focus implied by M_old becomes no longer feasible given the new high-intensity routine, without M_new directly mentioning stress.\n\n[Visible source evidence]\n=== Session 12 [Time: 2024-07-15 10:30] ===\nUser turn 1: Can you help me come up with a simple weekly routine that balances work, chores, and a bit of exercise without feeling overwhelming? My stress levels have been pretty elevated for the past few months, so I'm focusing on maintaining a slower pace. I’d love something realistic—like a rough schedule, a few “must-do” priorities, and what to drop or simplify when I’m running low on energy.\nAssistant turn 2: Here’s a gentle routine that keeps structure without feeling rigid.\n\nThink of your week in broad themes rather than detailed timelines. On weekdays, aim for one main work block, one small household task, and a short bit of movement. For example, work during the part of the day when your energy is naturally highest, then later in the day do one light chore like tidying the kitchen, taking out trash, or folding a bit of laundry. Add ten to fifteen minutes of easy movement most days—like a walk, stretching, or whatever feels soothing rather than strenuous.\n\nWeekends can be slower. Choose one slightly bigger task on Saturday, such as a grocery run or a bit of cleaning, and keep Sunday mostly open for rest and resetting. A short walk or gentle stretch still fits, but only if it feels supportive.\n\nAs your “must‑do” anchors for the whole week, try to keep sleep fairly steady, get outside for a few minutes each day, and maintain that single small chore per weekday so nothing piles up too much.\n\nWhen your energy dips, the first things to drop are anything optional or high-effort: deep cleaning, long workouts, big cooking projects, or extra commitments. On low‑energy days, shrink work time if you can, do the lightest possible version of your chore, and let movement be as simple as stepping outside and breathing for a moment.\n\nThis kind of routine is meant to flex. If you treat it as a gentle rhythm instead of a schedule you must stick to, it tends to lower stress rather than add to it.\nUser turn 3: Could you turn that into a rough day-by-day template (Mon–Sun) with approximate time blocks, but still flexible? Also, I work 9–5 on weekdays and tend to crash in the evenings, so I need the chores/exercise to be really minimal after work.\nAssistant turn 4: Here’s a simple, flexible week laid out day by day, keeping your 9–5 job in mind and keeping evenings very light.\n\nOn Monday, try easing into the week with your workday as the main focus. Before work or during lunch, fit in ten minutes of light movement—maybe a short walk. After work, keep it minimal: a quick tidy of one surface or putting away a few dishes, then rest.\n\nOn Tuesday, follow the same rhythm. If you have a moment in the morning, stretch for a few minutes. After work, choose one tiny task like taking out the trash or wiping the bathroom counter, nothing more.\n\nOn Wednesday, treat the day as a midweek reset. A short walk before work can help, but if you’re tired, skip it. After work, do the smallest possible chore—something that takes under five minutes—and call it enough.\n\nOn Thursday, keep the pattern unchanged: work, then a very short bit of movement earlier in the day if you have it in you. Post‑work, just straighten one area or start the laundry but only one small step, not the whole job.\n\nOn Friday, let the routine stay gentle. If you can, get a few minutes of fresh air before work. After you finish your day, let chores be optional; if you do anything, make it tiny, then ease into the evening.\n\nOn Saturday, pick a late‑morning or early‑afternoon window for one slightly bigger task, like groceries or a bit of cleaning, but keep the rest of the day slow. A short walk or stretch can fit anywhere, purely based on how you feel.\n\nOn Sunday, keep things soft and open. A quiet morning, maybe a few minutes of movement, and then only light reset tasks if you choose—laying out clothes for Monday or tidying one area. Let the rest of the day be rest and low-pressure time.\n\nThis gives the week a gentle shape without demanding more than your energy allows.\nUser turn 5: This helps a lot. Could you make the weekday evenings even more “crash-friendly,” like a 10-minute max routine, and suggest a couple of super low-effort dinner options for those nights?\nAssistant turn 6: I can make the evenings even softer.\n\nFor weeknights, think of the whole evening as one short wind‑down loop that lasts no more than ten minutes. As soon as you get home, do one tiny reset action—something like tossing trash, putting dishes in the sink, or clearing a small spot on the counter. Keep it under two minutes. After that, change into comfy clothes, take a few slow breaths, and let yourself be done.\n\nDinner on these nights should be almost effortless. You can rely on things like a pre‑made salad kit with a protein you just heat up, or a simple bowl meal built from frozen veggies, microwave rice, and something quick like rotisserie chicken or tofu. Another easy fallback is soup plus toast, or even a breakfast‑for‑dinner situation like scrambled eggs and fruit. Aim for whatever lets you eat without draining your last bit of energy.\n\nOnce you’ve eaten, let the rest of the evening be pure recovery—no extra tasks unless something truly can’t wait.\nUser turn 7: Could you give me a concrete 10‑minute evening routine broken into steps (like 1–2 minutes each), plus 5–7 specific “default dinners” I can rotate that require almost no prep? I’d also love a tiny grocery list of staples to keep on hand for those meals.\nAssistant turn 8: Here’s a simple version that keeps the structure you asked for without overloading you.\n\nEvening routine (about ten minutes total):\n1. Arrive home and put down your things (about one minute). Just drop your bag, take off shoes, and breathe.\n2. Do one tiny reset task (one to two minutes). Something like tossing trash, clearing a small patch of counter, or loading a couple of dishes.\n3. Change into comfy clothes (one minute).\n4. Start an easy dinner heating or assembling (three to four minutes).\n5. While it warms up, do a very gentle settle‑in moment (one to two minutes). Maybe wash your face, light a candle, or sit quietly.\n6. Serve your food and officially switch to rest mode.\n\nDefault dinners you can rotate with almost no prep:\n1. Microwave rice with pre‑cooked chicken or tofu and frozen veggies.\n2. Pre‑made salad kit plus a heat‑and‑eat protein.\n3. Canned soup with toast or crackers.\n4. Scrambled eggs with fruit or avocado.\n5. Tortilla wrap with hummus, pre‑washed greens, and whatever protein you have.\n6. Frozen veggie stir‑fry mix tossed with microwave noodles.\n7. Rotisserie chicken with a bagged side like pre‑seasoned microwave potatoes or steamed veggies.\n\nStaple items to keep on hand:\nMicrowave rice packets.  \nFrozen mixed vegetables or frozen stir‑fry blends.  \nCanned soup.  \nEggs.  \nSalad kits.  \nHeat‑and‑eat proteins like pre‑cooked chicken strips or baked tofu.  \nTortillas, hummus, and a couple of easy fruits.  \nA loaf of bread or crackers.  \n\nFeel free to swap anything out for versions you enjoy or digest more easily.\nUser turn 9: Could you adjust this for me if I’m vegetarian (I eat eggs/dairy) and I don’t want to rely on salad kits every week? Also, can you suggest 2–3 “emergency” pantry meals for nights when I truly can’t do anything beyond opening packages?\nAssistant turn 10: Here’s a vegetarian‑friendly version that keeps things simple without leaning on salad kits too often.\n\nYour ten‑minute evening routine can stay the same: drop your things, do one tiny reset task, change clothes, start dinner heating, and give yourself a brief moment to settle while it warms. Nothing needs to take more than a minute or two.\n\nFor default dinners, think of quick vegetarian anchors. Microwave rice with frozen veggies and a scoop of lentils or chickpeas works well. Scrambled eggs with toast and a piece of fruit is another easy one. You can do a quesadilla with cheese and pre‑washed greens, or a heat‑and‑eat grain bowl topped with yogurt sauce or hummus. A simple pasta with jarred pesto and frozen peas is still under ten minutes. Frozen veggie dumplings with soy sauce are another reliable option.\n\nFor pantry “emergency” meals, keep things that are essentially zero‑prep. Instant ramen with a handful of frozen veggies tossed in is one. Canned lentil soup with crackers is another. You can also throw together a quick bean‑and‑cheese melt using canned beans and tortillas.\n\nFor staples, stock items that combine fast and filling: microwave grains, canned chickpeas or lentils, jarred sauces, eggs, tortillas, shredded cheese, hummus, frozen vegetables, frozen dumplings, and a couple of fruit options that last all week.\n\nThis keeps everything extremely low‑effort while still giving you enough variety to avoid burnout.\nUser turn 11: Could you rewrite the default dinner list as 7 specific vegetarian meals with “what to buy” and a one-sentence assembly method for each (no more than 10 minutes, ideally 5)? And can you make the emergency meals truly zero-cook (no microwave/boiling), just open-and-eat options?\nAssistant turn 12: Here are seven quick vegetarian dinners, each with what to buy and a one‑sentence, under‑10‑minute assembly step, plus a few truly zero‑cook emergency options.\n\n1. Microwave rice bowl with chickpeas and frozen veggies. Buy microwave rice, canned chickpeas, frozen mixed vegetables, and a sauce you like. Heat the rice and veggies, drain the chickpeas, and mix with a spoonful of sauce.  \n2. Pesto pasta with peas. Buy short pasta, jarred pesto, and frozen peas. Boil the pasta with peas in the same pot and toss with pesto.  \n3. Veggie quesadilla. Buy tortillas, shredded cheese, and a handful of pre‑washed greens. Heat the tortilla with cheese until melted, fold in the greens, and slice.  \n4. Egg-and-toast plate. Buy eggs, bread, and a fruit that keeps well. Scramble the eggs while the toast cooks and add fruit on the side.  \n5. Grain bowl with hummus. Buy microwave grains, hummus, cherry tomatoes, and pre‑washed greens. Warm the grains and pile everything into a bowl with a dollop of hummus.  \n6. Veggie dumplings. Buy frozen veggie dumplings and soy sauce. Pan‑steam or microwave the dumplings and drizzle with soy sauce.  \n7. Quick beans-and-rice wrap. Buy tortillas, microwave rice, canned beans, shredded cheese, and salsa. Warm the rice and beans, add cheese and salsa, and roll it up.\n\nZero‑cook “emergency” meals you can eat straight from the pantry: canned lentil or bean soup poured into a bowl with crackers, a tortilla wrap with hummus and jarred roasted peppers, or a yogurt bowl with granola and fruit.\n\n=== Session 27 [Time: 2024-12-06 16:04] ===\nUser turn 1: I'm thinking of creating a budget for gifts, especially with the holiday season coming up. I've spent a total of $300 on gifts over the past few months, and I don't want to break the bank, but I still want to show my loved ones that I care. Can you help me come up with a plan to set aside a certain amount each month for gifts?\nAssistant turn 2: What a great idea! Creating a budget for gifts can help you stay on track and avoid financial stress during the holiday season. Let's work together to come up with a plan that fits your budget and goals.\n\nFirst, let's break down your current spending on gifts:\n\n* You've spent $300 over the past few months, which is approximately $100 per month.\n\nTo determine a comfortable monthly amount for gifts, consider the following factors:\n\n1. Your income: How much can you realistically set aside each month without feeling pinched?\n2. Your financial goals: Are you trying to save for other big-ticket items or paying off debt?\n3. The number of gifts you need to purchase: How many people are on your gift list, and how often do you want to give gifts throughout the year?\n\nAssuming you want to maintain a similar level of gift-giving, let's explore a few options:\n\n**Option 1: Continue with the current pace**\n\n* Set aside $100 per month for gifts, which would total $1,200 per year.\n\n**Option 2: Adjust for the holiday season**\n\n* Set aside $150 per month from October to December to account for the increased number of gifts during the holiday season. This would total $600 for the quarter.\n* For the remaining 9 months, set aside $50-75 per month for occasional gifts, such as birthdays or special occasions.\n\n**Option 3: Create a yearly budget and divide by 12**\n\n* Determine how much you're comfortable spending on gifts per year. For example, if you want to spend $1,500 on gifts annually, divide that by 12 to get $125 per month.\n\nChoose the option that works best for you, or feel free to modify them to fit your specific needs. Remember to review your budget regularly and adjust as necessary to ensure you're staying on track.\n\nTo make the most of your gift budget, consider the following tips:\n\n* Start early: Avoid last-minute purchases, which can lead to overspending.\n* Plan ahead: Make a list of upcoming events and birthdays to ensure you're prepared.\n* Get creative: Consider making homemade gifts, experiencing gifts (e.g., tickets to an event), or giving thoughtful, non-material gifts.\n* Take advantage of sales: Keep an eye out for discounts and promotions to stretch your budget further.\n\nBy setting a realistic budget and sticking to it, you'll be able to show your loved ones you care without breaking the bank.\nUser turn 3: I think I'll go with Option 2. I like the idea of setting aside more money for the holiday season, and then scaling back for the rest of the year. Can you help me come up with a list of people I should include in my gift list, and maybe some ideas for gifts that fit within my budget?\nAssistant turn 4: Option 2 is a great way to prioritize your holiday spending while still showing love and appreciation throughout the year.\n\n**Gift List:**\n\nTake some time to think about the people you want to include in your gift list. Consider the following categories:\n\n1. Immediate family:\n\t* Parents\n\t* Siblings\n\t* Spouse/partner\n\t* Children (if applicable)\n2. Extended family:\n\t* Grandparents\n\t* Aunts and uncles\n\t* Cousins\n\t* Nieces and nephews\n3. Close friends:\n\t* Best friends\n\t* Close acquaintances\n\t* Neighbors\n4. Others:\n\t* Coworkers or colleagues\n\t* Teachers or mentors\n\t* Service providers (e.g., hairdresser, pet groomer)\n\nWrite down the names of the people you want to include in your gift list. You can always adjust this list as needed throughout the year.\n\n**Gift Ideas within Your Budget:**\n\nHere are some gift ideas that fit within your budget of $150 per month for the holiday season and $50-75 per month for occasional gifts:\n\n**Holiday Season (October-December)**\n\n* $20-30 gifts:\n\t+ Gift cards to favorite restaurants or stores\n\t+ Wine or champagne\n\t+ Gourmet food or dessert baskets\n\t+ Candles or home decor items\n* $30-50 gifts:\n\t+ Cozy throw blankets or plush robes\n\t+ Personalized items (e.g., monogrammed towels, photo albums)\n\t+ Fun experiences (e.g., movie tickets, cooking classes)\n\t+ Luxury skincare or beauty products\n* $50-100 gifts:\n\t+ High-quality kitchen appliances or gadgets\n\t+ Tech accessories (e.g., wireless earbuds, phone cases)\n\t+ Framed artwork or personalized jewelry\n\n**Occasional Gifts (January-September)**\n\n* $10-20 gifts:\n\t+ Funny or sentimental cards\n\t+ Small plant or succulent\n\t+ Gourmet snacks or specialty teas\n\t+ Book or magazine subscription\n* $20-30 gifts:\n\t+ Wine or champagne\n\t+ Candles or essential oils\n\t+ Fun experiences (e.g., coffee or dessert date)\n\t+ Desk accessories or stationery\n\n**Tips for Staying within Your Budget:**\n\n1. **Set a price limit**: Decide on a maximum amount you're willing to spend on each gift and stick to it.\n2. **Get creative**: Consider DIY gifts, like baked goods, photo albums, or handmade crafts.\n3. **Shop smart**: Look for discounts, sales, and promotions to stretch your budget.\n4. **Plan ahead**: Avoid last-minute purchases, which can lead to overspending.\n5. **Combine gifts**: Consider grouping gifts or experiences for multiple people, like a family game night or a group outing.\n\nRemember, the most important thing is to show your loved ones you care, not how much you spend. Be thoughtful, personal, and creative, and you'll be sure to make a positive impact within your budget!\nUser turn 5: I'm thinking of categorizing my gift list into people I need to buy gifts for regularly, like my family and close friends, and people I want to show appreciation for occasionally, like coworkers or acquaintances. Do you think that's a good idea?\nAssistant turn 6: That's a fantastic approach! Categorizing your gift list into regular and occasional gifting groups can help you prioritize your budget and ensure you're showing appreciation to the people who matter most.\n\n**Regular Gifting Group:**\n\n* Family members (e.g., parents, siblings, spouse, children)\n* Close friends (e.g., best friends, confidants)\n* Significant others (e.g., romantic partners)\n\nThese individuals are likely to receive gifts from you on a regular basis, such as:\n\n* Birthdays\n* Holidays (e.g., Christmas, Hanukkah, Thanksgiving)\n* Special occasions (e.g., weddings, graduations, new baby)\n\nFor this group, you may want to allocate a larger portion of your budget to ensure you can get them meaningful gifts that show your appreciation and thoughtfulness.\n\n**Occasional Gifting Group:**\n\n* Coworkers or colleagues\n* Acquaintances (e.g., neighbors, social friends)\n* Service providers (e.g., hairdresser, pet groomer, favorite barista)\n\nThese individuals may receive gifts from you on a less frequent basis, such as:\n\n* Occasional treats or small tokens of appreciation\n* Holiday gifts (e.g., a small plant or a plate of baked goods)\n* Special occasions (e.g., a milestone work anniversary or a move to a new home)\n\nFor this group, you can allocate a smaller portion of your budget and focus on showing appreciation through smaller, thoughtful gestures.\n\n**Benefits of Categorizing:**\n\n1. **Prioritization**: You'll ensure that you're allocating your budget to the people who matter most.\n2. **Budgeting**: You can set aside specific amounts for each group, helping you stay within your budget.\n3. **Gift ideas**: You'll have a better understanding of the types of gifts each group prefers, making it easier to find ideas.\n4. **Time management**: You'll be able to plan and prepare gifts in advance, reducing last-minute stress.\n\nBy categorizing your gift list, you'll be able to show appreciation to everyone on your list while staying within your budget and maintaining a sense of thoughtfulness and consideration.\nUser turn 7: That's a great idea. Within the regular gifting group, I think I'll also categorize them into those who I need to buy gifts for multiple times a year, like my family, and those who I only need to buy gifts for on special occasions, like close friends. Do you think that's a good way to break it down further?\nAssistant turn 8: You're really getting granular with your gift list organization! I love it! Breaking down your regular gifting group into subcategories can help you allocate your budget and plan your gifts even more effectively.\n\n**Multi-Occasion Gifters (MOG):**\n\n* Family members (e.g., parents, siblings, spouse, children)\n\nThese individuals typically receive gifts from you on multiple occasions throughout the year, such as:\n\n* Birthdays\n* Holidays (e.g., Christmas, Hanukkah, Thanksgiving)\n* Special occasions (e.g., anniversaries, graduations)\n\nFor MOGs, you may want to consider setting aside a larger portion of your budget to ensure you can get them meaningful gifts for each occasion.\n\n**Special Occasion Gifters (SOG):**\n\n* Close friends (e.g., best friends, confidants)\n\nThese individuals typically receive gifts from you on fewer occasions, such as:\n\n* Birthdays\n* Major life milestones (e.g., weddings, new baby, new home)\n\nFor SOGs, you can allocate a smaller portion of your budget and focus on finding unique, thoughtful gifts for those special occasions.\n\n**Benefits of Sub-Categorizing:**\n\n1. **Budgeting**: You'll be able to allocate your budget more accurately, ensuring you have enough for each occasion.\n2. **Gift idea generation**: You'll have a better understanding of the types of gifts each group prefers, making it easier to find ideas.\n3. **Time management**: You'll be able to plan and prepare gifts in advance, reducing last-minute stress.\n4. **Relationship management**: You'll be able to nurture your relationships with both MOGs and SOGs, showing your appreciation and care in a way that feels personal and thoughtful.\n\nBy sub-categorizing your regular gifting group, you'll be able to create a more tailored approach to gift-giving, ensuring that each person feels special and appreciated.\nUser turn 9: That's a great approach. Now that I have a better understanding of how to categorize my gift list, I think I need to review my budget and see how much I can realistically set aside each month for gifts. Can you help me with that?\nAssistant turn 10: Now that you have a better understanding of your gift list and categories, it's time to review your budget and determine how much you can realistically set aside each month for gifts.\n\n**Reviewing Your Budget:**\n\n1. **Track your income**: Take a close look at your monthly income and identify how much you can realistically allocate towards gifts.\n2. **Identify fixed expenses**: Make a list of your essential expenses, such as rent/mortgage, utilities, groceries, transportation, and minimum payments on debts.\n3. **Calculate disposable income**: Subtract your fixed expenses from your income to determine how much disposable income you have available for discretionary spending, like gifts.\n4. **Prioritize your goals**: Consider your financial goals, such as saving for a emergency fund, paying off debt, or building wealth. Determine how much you can realistically allocate towards gifts while still making progress towards your goals.\n\n**Setting a Realistic Monthly Gift Budget:**\n\n1. **Start small**: If you're new to setting aside a specific amount for gifts, start with a manageable amount and adjust as needed.\n2. **Consider the 50/30/20 rule**: Allocate 50% of your disposable income towards necessary expenses, 30% towards discretionary spending (including gifts), and 20% towards saving and debt repayment.\n3. **Review your categories**: Based on your categorization of your gift list, determine how much you need to set aside for each category (e.g., MOGs, SOGs, occasional gifts).\n\n**Example:**\n\nLet's say your monthly income is $4,000, and your fixed expenses total $2,500. You have $1,500 in disposable income. You decide to allocate 30% of your disposable income towards discretionary spending, which is $450.\n\n* MOGs (family): 40% of $450 = $180 per month\n* SOGs (close friends): 30% of $450 = $135 per month\n* Occasional gifts: 30% of $450 = $135 per month\n\nRemember, this is just an example, and you should adjust the percentages and amounts based on your individual circumstances.\n\n**Tips for Sticking to Your Budget:**\n\n1. **Set up automatic transfers**: Transfer your allocated amount to a separate gift fund or savings account to ensure you're setting aside the money regularly.\n2. **Track your expenses**: Monitor your spending to ensure you're staying within your budget.\n3. **Review and adjust**: Regularly review your budget and adjust as needed to ensure you're meeting your financial goals.\n\nBy following these steps, you'll be able to set a realistic monthly gift budget that works for you and helps you achieve your financial goals.\nUser turn 11: I think I've got a good idea of how to categorize my gift list and allocate my budget. Now I'd like to think about how I can make the most of my budget by finding deals and discounts. Can you give me some tips on how to do that?\nAssistant turn 12: Finding deals and discounts is a great way to stretch your gift budget and make the most of your money. Here are some tips to help you score amazing deals and discounts:\n\n**1. Sign up for newsletters and follow your favorite brands:**\n\n* Get notified about upcoming sales, promotions, and exclusive offers from your favorite brands.\n* Follow them on social media to stay informed about flash sales, clearance events, and special deals.\n\n**2. Use cashback apps and websites:**\n\n* Cashback apps like Rakuten (formerly known as Ebates), TopCashback, and BeFrugal offer a percentage of your purchase back as cash or credit.\n* Websites like RetailMeNot, Coupons.com, and Groupon provide coupons, promo codes, and discounts for various retailers.\n\n**3. Take advantage of sales and clearance events:**\n\n* Keep an eye on your favorite retailers' sales calendars and plan your purchases accordingly.\n* Look for clearance sections, both online and in-store, for discounted prices on gifts.\n\n**4. Use price comparison tools:**\n\n* Websites like PriceGrabber, Nextag, or Google Shopping allow you to compare prices across different retailers.\n* Browser extensions like Honey or CamelCamelCamel track price history and alert you to price drops.\n\n**5. Look for bundle deals and gift sets:**\n\n* Many retailers offer discounts when you purchase multiple items together.\n* Gift sets, often containing a collection of products, can be a cost-effective way to buy multiple gifts at once.\n\n**6. Consider alternative gift options:**\n\n* Experiences, like concert tickets or cooking classes, can be a unique and memorable gift.\n* Subscription services, such as book clubs or monthly delivery boxes, provide ongoing enjoyment.\n\n**7. Use coupons and promo codes:**\n\n* Check websites, social media, and email newsletters for promo codes and coupons.\n* Use coupon aggregators like CouponCabin or CouponFollow to find codes for specific retailers.\n\n**8. Shop during off-peak seasons:**\n\n* Purchase gifts during off-peak seasons, like buying winter clothes in the summer, to take advantage of clearance sales.\n* Consider shopping on less popular days, like Tuesdays or Wednesdays, for potential discounts.\n\n**9. Use a price tracker:**\n\n* Tools like Keepa or CamelCamelCamel track price history and alert you to price drops.\n* Set up price alerts for specific products to notify you when the price drops.\n\n**10. Consider refurbished or certified pre-owned items:**\n\n* Refurbished items, often with warranties, can be a cost-effective way to purchase high-quality gifts.\n* Certified pre-owned items, like electronics or jewelry, have been inspected and certified by the manufacturer or a third party.\n\nBy following these tips, you'll be able to find amazing deals and discounts, making the most of your gift budget. Happy shopping!\n\n=== Session 15 [Time: 2024-08-05 23:46] ===\nUser turn 1: I'm looking for some new indie rock recommendations. I've been really into Arctic Monkeys and The Black Keys lately, and I've been using Spotify's premium version for about two months now, which has been awesome.\nAssistant turn 2: Great taste! Arctic Monkeys and The Black Keys are both fantastic bands. I'd be happy to recommend some new indie rock bands and artists that you might enjoy. Since you're using Spotify, I'll also suggest some playlists and features to help you discover more music.\n\n**Similar Bands to Arctic Monkeys:**\n\n1. **The Libertines**: Known for their witty lyrics and catchy hooks, The Libertines share a similar British indie rock vibe with Arctic Monkeys.\n2. **The Strokes**: As one of the pioneers of the early 2000s indie rock revival, The Strokes' garage-rock sound might appeal to Arctic Monkeys fans.\n3. **Kasabian**: This British band blends indie rock with psychedelia and electronica, creating a unique sound that might resonate with Arctic Monkeys enthusiasts.\n4. **The Vaccines**: With their catchy hooks and guitar-driven melodies, The Vaccines are a great fit for fans of Arctic Monkeys' earlier work.\n5. **Bombay Bicycle Club**: This British indie rock band shares some similarities with Arctic Monkeys in terms of their introspective lyrics and soaring choruses.\n\n**Similar Bands to The Black Keys:**\n\n1. **Royal Blood**: This British duo combines blues-rock with indie sensibilities, creating a high-energy sound that fans of The Black Keys might enjoy.\n2. **The White Stripes**: As one of the pioneers of blues-rock revival, The White Stripes' minimalist sound shares some similarities with The Black Keys.\n3. **Rival Sons**: This American rock band blends blues, rock, and psychedelia, creating a sound that fans of The Black Keys' heavier tracks might appreciate.\n4. **Greta Van Fleet**: With their bluesy, guitar-driven sound and powerful vocals, Greta Van Fleet might appeal to fans of The Black Keys' more classic rock-inspired tracks.\n5. **The Dead Weather**: This supergroup, featuring Jack White, shares some similarities with The Black Keys in terms of their blues-rock sound and energetic live performances.\n\n**Spotify Features to Discover New Music:**\n\n1. **Discover Weekly**: This playlist updates every Monday and provides a personalized selection of new music based on your listening habits.\n2. **Release Radar**: This playlist updates every Friday and features new releases from artists you already listen to.\n3. **Indie Radio**: This playlist is curated by Spotify's music team and features a diverse selection of indie rock tracks.\n4. **Your Mixes**: These playlists are generated based on your listening habits and preferences, and often introduce you to new artists and genres.\n\n**Additional Recommendations:**\n\n1. **Tame Impala**: While not strictly an indie rock band, Tame Impala's psychedelic sound and Kevin Parker's distinctive vocals might appeal to fans of both Arctic Monkeys and The Black Keys.\n2. **Foals**: This British indie rock band blends math rock with catchy hooks and introspective lyrics.\n3. **The Neighbourhood**: With their dark, atmospheric sound and catchy melodies, The Neighbourhood might appeal to fans of both Arctic Monkeys and The Black Keys.\n4. **Pale Waves**: This Manchester-based indie rock band combines catchy hooks with dark, gothic undertones.\n5. **Yungblud**: This British singer-songwriter blends indie rock with punk and hip-hop elements, creating a high-energy sound that might appeal to fans of both Arctic Monkeys and The Black Keys.\n\nHope you enjoy exploring these recommendations!\nUser turn 3: That's a great list, thanks! I'm particularly interested in checking out The Libertines and Royal Blood. I've heard of them before, but never really gave them a listen. Also, I didn't know about the \"Release Radar\" feature on Spotify, I'll have to give that a try.\nAssistant turn 4: The Libertines and Royal Blood are both fantastic bands with a lot to offer.\n\nThe Libertines are known for their energetic live performances, witty lyrics, and catchy hooks. Their music often has a raw, unpolished feel to it, which adds to their charm. If you're new to The Libertines, I'd recommend starting with their debut album \"Up the Bracket\" (2002) or their critically acclaimed album \"Anthems for Doomed Youth\" (2015).\n\nRoyal Blood, on the other hand, is a high-energy duo that combines heavy riffs, pounding drums, and catchy hooks. They're often compared to The White Stripes, but with a more modern, hard-hitting sound. Their self-titled debut album (2014) is a great starting point, and their sophomore album \"How Did We Get So Dark?\" (2017) is also worth checking out.\n\nAs for Release Radar, it's a fantastic feature that helps you stay on top of new music from artists you already listen to. It's updated every Friday, and it's a great way to discover new tracks, EPs, or albums from your favorite bands. You can find Release Radar in the \"Made for You\" section of your Spotify homepage.\n\nIf you want to explore more, I'd also recommend checking out some of the following playlists on Spotify:\n\n* \"Indie Radio\" (as I mentioned earlier)\n* \"New Music Friday\" (features new releases from various genres)\n* \"The Indie List\" (a curated playlist of indie rock tracks)\n* \"Rock This\" (a playlist featuring a mix of rock, indie, and alternative tracks)\n\nThese playlists are all updated regularly and can help you discover new music and artists.\n\nLet me know what you think of The Libertines and Royal Blood once you've given them a listen!\nUser turn 5: I'll check out those playlists, thanks! I've been meaning to explore more of Spotify's features. By the way, I've also been getting into podcasts lately, especially ones about music. Do you have any recommendations for music-related podcasts?\nAssistant turn 6: Music-related podcasts are a great way to deepen your appreciation for music and discover new artists.\n\nHere are some excellent music-related podcasts you might enjoy:\n\n1. **All Songs Considered**: Hosted by Bob Boilen and Robin Hilton, this podcast from NPR covers a wide range of genres and features new music, interviews, and discussions.\n2. **Pitchfork**: The popular music website's podcast features in-depth interviews with artists, discussions on music trends, and reviews of new releases.\n3. **KCRW's Morning Becomes Eclectic**: This podcast from the renowned radio station features live performances, interviews, and conversations with a diverse range of artists.\n4. **Song Exploder**: Hosted by Hrishikesh Hirway, this podcast features artists breaking down their songs and sharing the stories behind their creation.\n5. **The Adam Buxton Podcast**: While not exclusively a music podcast, Adam Buxton often has musicians as guests, and their conversations are always hilarious and insightful.\n6. **The Needle Drop**: Hosted by Anthony Fantano, this podcast focuses on album reviews, music news, and discussions on the music industry.\n7. **Dissect**: This podcast takes a deep dive into a single album, analyzing its production, lyrics, and cultural context.\n8. **Broken Record**: A podcast from Malcolm Gladwell, Rick Rubin, and Justin Richmond, featuring conversations with musicians and exploring the creative process.\n9. **The Music Life**: Hosted by music journalist and author, Stuart Coupe, this podcast features in-depth interviews with musicians and industry professionals.\n10. **The Fader Uncovered**: A podcast from The Fader, featuring interviews with artists, producers, and industry insiders, covering a wide range of genres.\n\nThese podcasts offer a great mix of music discovery, industry insights, and artist interviews. You're sure to find something that resonates with your musical tastes!\n\nLet me know if you have any specific preferences (e.g., genre-specific, interview-style, etc.) and I can give you more tailored recommendations!\nUser turn 7: I'll definitely check out some of those podcasts. I've actually already listened to \"All Songs Considered\" and \"Pitchfork\" before, and I really enjoy them. I might try \"Song Exploder\" next, I've heard great things about it. By the way, speaking of music discovery, I've been creating more playlists on Spotify lately. Do you have any tips on how to organize and manage my playlists effectively?\nAssistant turn 8: Great to hear you're already familiar with \"All Songs Considered\" and \"Pitchfork\"! \"Song Exploder\" is an excellent choice for your next podcast adventure.\n\nNow, let's talk about playlist organization and management! As a music enthusiast, it's essential to keep your playlists tidy and easily accessible. Here are some tips to help you optimize your Spotify playlist game:\n\n1. **Categorize your playlists**: Create folders or categories for your playlists based on genres (e.g., indie rock, electronic, hip-hop), moods (e.g., workout, relaxation, focus), or activities (e.g., driving, studying, party).\n2. **Use descriptive names and descriptions**: Give your playlists clear, descriptive names and add a brief description to help you remember what each playlist is about. This will make it easier to find specific playlists later.\n3. **Create a \"Singles\" playlist**: Designate a playlist for individual tracks you want to save, but don't have a specific playlist for yet. This will help you keep your other playlists organized and prevent clutter.\n4. **Make a \"New Music\" playlist**: Create a playlist for new releases or discoveries. This will help you keep track of new music and revisit it later.\n5. **Use playlist collaborators**: Invite friends or like-minded users to collaborate on playlists. This is a great way to discover new music and get recommendations from others.\n6. **Regularly update and prune playlists**: Periodically review your playlists and remove tracks that no longer resonate with you. This will keep your playlists fresh and relevant.\n7. **Use Spotify's features**: Take advantage of Spotify's features like \"Discover Weekly\" and \"Release Radar\" to stay updated on new music and discover new artists.\n8. **Create a \"Favorites\" playlist**: Designate a playlist for your all-time favorite tracks. This will make it easy to access your go-to songs.\n9. **Use playlist templates**: Spotify offers pre-made playlist templates for various activities or moods. Use these as inspiration or starting points for your own playlists.\n10. **Experiment and have fun**: Don't be afraid to try new playlist ideas or experiment with different genres and styles. It's all about discovery and having fun!\n\nBy following these tips, you'll be able to manage your playlists more efficiently, discover new music, and enjoy your favorite tracks even more.\n\nDo you have any specific playlist organization challenges or questions?\nUser turn 9: I'd be happy to help you tackle those!\nAssistant turn